# $project_name

A Contexture MCP server. It declares one Application, one Role, one Skill, and
one Tool. A Skill tells an agent how to work; a Tool is typed Python code that
Contexture runs.

## Run and debug locally

```bash
uv sync
uv run contexture check
uv run contexture list
uv run contexture inspect $role_ref/check-target
uv run contexture call $role_ref/ping --input '{"target":"example.test"}'
uv run contexture serve
```

`serve` speaks MCP over stdio and blocks. The preceding commands do not need a
Host such as Claude Code or Codex.

## Connect a host

The host launches the same command:

```bash
claude mcp add --scope project $project_name -- uv run contexture serve
codex  mcp add                 $project_name -- uv run contexture serve
```

## Where to write your own code

```text
$project_name/
├── pyproject.toml       points to the one exported application
├── $package_name/
│   ├── __init__.py      app = Contexture(...)
│   ├── role.py          responsibility boundary          Role
│   ├── skills.py        procedure the model follows      Skill
│   └── tools.py         typed capability Contexture runs Tool
└── uv.lock              created by uv sync
```

Add child Roles, Skills, and Tools in this package. When the application needs
external connections, a human-triggered Prompt, or a host-readable Resource,
add those declarations to the same `app` object.
